<TopicPage slug="subjects/nuclear-engineering/foundational/radiation-physics-and-detection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nuclear engineering is built on the physics of unstable nuclei: which nuclei decay, what
    they emit, how those emissions interact with matter, and how the interactions are detected
    and measured. Everything else in the discipline — reactor criticality, shielding design,
    isotope production, waste policy — is this foundation applied. The physics has a peculiar
    and important character: individual atomic events are utterly random (no one can say when a
    particular atom will decay), yet the <em>statistics</em> of trillions of atoms are the most
    precise laws in engineering. Learning to think in ensembles rather than atoms is the
    discipline's first intellectual leap, and it happens on day one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does an unstable nucleus decay at all? The nucleus is a tug-of-war: the strong nuclear
    force binds protons and neutrons together, while electrical repulsion pushes the protons
    apart. For light elements, stability favours roughly equal numbers of each (carbon-12: six
    and six); as elements get heavier, extra neutrons are needed as nuclear "glue", and past
    bismuth-209 no configuration is stable at all. When a nucleus has the wrong neutron-to-
    proton ratio — or simply too much mass — it has an excess of energy, and nature sheds that
    energy by emitting particles and radiation. Those emissions are what this page tracks:
    first the decays themselves, then what the emitted radiation does to matter, then how
    matter is turned into measurement.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decay, penetration, detection — and dose" widget="equationStep" config={radiationCfg} />

  <h2>Radioactive decay: the modes and the law</h2>
  <p>
    The decay modes sort by what the nucleus is trying to fix. <strong>Alpha decay</strong>
    ejects a helium-4 nucleus (two protons, two neutrons, a very tightly bound package) — the
    route taken by heavy nuclei such as uranium-238 and radium-226, which reduce both their
    mass and their proton count in one move. <strong>Beta-minus decay</strong> fixes a neutron
    excess: a neutron converts to a proton, emitting an electron and an antineutrino
    (carbon-14 → nitrogen-14 is the famous case, and the basis of radiocarbon dating).
    <strong>Beta-plus decay</strong> and <strong>electron capture</strong> fix the opposite
    problem — a proton excess — by converting a proton to a neutron (fluorine-18 decaying by
    positron emission is the engine of PET imaging). <strong>Gamma emission</strong> usually
    follows the others: the daughter nucleus is born in an excited state and sheds pure
    electromagnetic energy — no particle, no change of identity, just photons. When a nucleus
    is extremely neutron-rich it can even emit a neutron outright, which is why fission
    products chain into delayed-neutron physics later in reactor theory.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="three half-lives, three timelines" widget="plotter" config={decayCfg} />
  <p>
    The timing of all this is governed by one law, and it is the discipline's most relentless
    curve. Decay is a constant-probability event: each nucleus of a given species has a fixed
    chance λ of decaying per unit time, independent of its age, temperature, pressure or
    chemistry. For a population N, that gives dN/dt = −λN, hence the exponential
    N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−λt</sup>, and its friendly face, the <strong>half-life</strong>
    (t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693/λ): the time for half of any sample to decay. Activity — decays per
    second, measured in becquerels (Bq; the older curie equals 3.7 × 10<sup>10</sup> Bq) —
    follows the same curve, since A = λN. The plot above shows three exponentials and thereby
    three nuclear personalities: iodine-131 (8 days) is intensely radioactive and briefly so —
    ideal for treating thyroid tumours and gone in months; caesium-137 (30 years) decays
    slowly enough to define the decades-long contamination legacy of Chernobyl and Fukushima;
    and a 72-second nuclide is effectively gone before you can measure it twice. A worked
    example sets the arithmetic: a source of I-131 held for 32 days has passed four
    half-lives, so 1/2<sup>4</sup> = 1/16 — about 6 per cent — of the initial activity
    remains; the same hold-up applied to caesium-137 removes almost nothing. Multiply this
    one curve by the thousands of nuclides in spent nuclear fuel and you have decay heat, the
    reason spent fuel sits in cooling pools for years and dry casks for decades — the entire
    waste-timeline debate is exponentials arguing with each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heavy nuclides rarely decay in one step. Uranium-238 heads a <strong>decay chain</strong>:
    fourteen successive emissions — eight alphas and six betas — through radium-226 and the
    gas radon-222 down to stable lead-206. Chains create subtleties that matter later: a
    daughter's activity can grow while the parent decays, and in <strong>secular
    equilibrium</strong> (parent half-life vastly longer) the chain members all decay at the
    parent's rate — which is why old uranium ore is a cocktail of the whole chain, and why
    radon (a gas that escapes rock into basements) is a decay-chain link with public-health
    weight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "radioactive" describes a substance, full stop — so a more radioactive thing is automatically more dangerous. Radioactivity is an event rate, not a substance property, and hazard depends on what is emitted, at what energy, and where the source sits relative to the body. A curie of tritium (a weak beta emitter) is a lab curiosity; a curie of caesium-137 (a strong gamma emitter) is a severe incident. The number on the Geiger counter is the beginning of the safety analysis, not its conclusion.</p>

  <h2>Interactions of radiation with matter</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation is classified by what it is, and the classification dictates both harm and
    shielding. <strong>Charged particles</strong> lose energy continuously through electrical
    interactions with atomic electrons, ionising as they go. <strong>Alphas</strong> are heavy
    (about 7,300 electron masses) and doubly charged, so they ionise ferociously but brake fast:
    a 5 MeV alpha travels a few centimetres in air and is stopped by a sheet of paper — or by
    the dead outer layer of skin. Outside the body, an alpha source is almost harmless; inside
    (inhaled radon daughters, ingested polonium-210) it deposits all its energy in a few
    microns of living tissue — the most locally damaging radiation there is, a contrast
    engineers capture in the term LET (linear energy transfer). <strong>Betas</strong> are
    light and fast, travelling metres in air and millimetres to centimetres in tissue, stopped
    by a few millimetres of plastic; their one shielding subtlety is that braking in dense,
    high-Z material (lead) produces secondary X-rays (bremsstrahlung), so beta shields are
    plastic or aluminium, with lead only behind them if gammas follow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gamma rays and X-rays</strong> — photons, uncharged — interact in three ways whose
    balance depends on energy and material. At low energies the <strong>photoelectric
    effect</strong> dominates: the photon is absorbed wholesale, ejecting an electron (scaling
    steeply with atomic number — the physics behind both lead shielding and medical X-ray
    contrast). At middle energies <strong>Compton scattering</strong>: the photon bounces off
    an electron, losing part of its energy and changing direction — the main hazard mechanism
    in shielding design, since scattered photons continue as a degraded beam. Above 1.022 MeV,
    <strong>pair production</strong>: the photon converts into an electron and a positron in
    the nuclear field. Because all three remove photons stochastically from a beam, gamma
    attenuation is exponential: I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−μx</sup>, with each half-value layer
    of material halving the intensity — there is no "stopping" thickness, only attenuation to
    an accepted fraction, which is why concrete walls and layered lead are specified in
    half-value layers, and why a hundred HVLs (a factor of 10<sup>30</sup> reduction) is a
    design number, not a fantasy. <strong>Neutrons</strong>, being uncharged, ignore electron
    clouds and interact only with nuclei: they scatter (best off nuclei of similar mass —
    hydrogen, hence water, plastic and concrete as moderators), get captured (boron-10 and
    cadmium are legendary absorbers), and can activate stable materials into radioactive
    ones — the reason the steel of a reactor vessel outlives the reactor's usefulness as a
    radioactive object. Shielding a neutron source is therefore a sandwich: hydrogenous
    material to slow the neutrons, boron to absorb them, and lead or concrete behind to catch
    the capture gammas.
  </p>

  <h2>Detection and measurement: turning physics into numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Every detector exploits the same fact: radiation ionises and excites matter, and those
    ionisation events can be counted. <strong>Gas-filled detectors</strong> differ by the
    voltage across the chamber. An <em>ionisation chamber</em> collects the primary ion pairs
    directly, giving a current proportional to dose — the instrument of dose-rate surveys. A
    <em>proportional counter</em> runs at higher voltage so each ion pair seeds a small
    avalanche, amplifying the signal while preserving energy information. A <em>Geiger–Müller
    tube</em> runs higher still: every interaction triggers a full avalanche, so every event
    clicks identically — superb sensitivity and simplicity, no energy information, and a
    characteristic dead time after each pulse. <strong>Scintillators</strong> take the other
    route: materials such as NaI(Tl) emit flashes of light when radiation deposits energy in
    them, a photomultiplier converts each flash to an electrical pulse, and the pulse's
    <em>height</em> reveals the deposited energy — gamma spectroscopy, which identifies
    nuclides by their photon signatures (the 662 keV line of caesium-137 is the standard's
    standard). <strong>Semiconductor detectors</strong> (high-purity germanium) push energy
    resolution far further — resolving gamma lines that NaI only smears together — at the
    price of cryogenic cooling. <strong>Neutrons</strong>, invisible to all of the above,
    demand converters: boron-trifluoride or helium-3 tubes counting the charged fragments of
    the neutron-boron reaction, fission chambers, or hydrogen-rich scintillators reading
    recoil protons. Personal dosimetry completes the picture — thermoluminescent and
    optically stimulated dosimeters accumulating dose like exposure film, read monthly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because emission is random, every measurement is a sample from a Poisson distribution, and
    the statistical uncertainty of a count N is approximately √N. Count 100 events and the
    uncertainty is ±10 per cent; count 10,000 and it is ±1 per cent. A decade of lab students
    discovering that "my count rate disagrees with theory" has rediscovered this — the answer
    is usually not a broken instrument but an under-counted average. Competent nuclear
    measurement therefore always reports the error bars, and competent reactor operators,
    health physicists and safeguards inspectors all live by the same discipline: radiation
    detection is statistics with instrumentation, never certainty with a needle.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper threads show where the foundation leads. <strong>Decay heat</strong>: a
    shutdown reactor does not switch off — the decaying fission products continue to generate
    roughly 6–7 per cent of full power immediately after scram, falling as the short-lived
    nuclides exhaust themselves. This single consequence of the exponential law sized the
    emergency cooling systems of every reactor and drove the Fukushima accident's core-isolation
    dynamics; the accident's ultimate lesson was about decay heat and backup power, not
    criticality. <strong>Activity versus dose</strong>: connecting Bq to sieverts runs through
    emission energy, decay branching, interaction physics, biological weighting and geometry —
    the chain of conversion factors that health physics as a profession maintains; a handful
    of numbers (1 Gy = 1 J/kg, quality factors, tissue weighting factors) carries the entire
    protection framework, and the ICRP's linear-no-threshold assumption for stochastic risk
    remains the field's most consequential and most debated scientific commitment.
    <strong>Detection limits and metrology</strong>: real measurements face backgrounds
    (cosmic rays, radon daughters, the detector's own noise), so a "detection" is a statistical
    decision with a minimum detectable activity — the arithmetic behind safeguards
    declarations and environmental surveys alike — and every survey meter's credibility
    descends from national standards laboratories through calibrated sources in an unbroken
    chain of traceability. Applications radiate outward from the same physics: carbon-14
    dating (5,730-year half-life, decay clock), PET imaging (positron annihilation's two
    511 keV gammas flown in opposite directions), neutron activation analysis identifying
    trace elements, and smoke detectors (americium-241 ionising a detection chamber). The
    discipline's promise to a student is unusual: learn one curve and three interactions, and
    you can read reactor safety cases, hospital physics, environmental monitoring and
    non-proliferation — they are all this page, wearing different overalls.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET nuclear program criteria; Krane, <em>Introductory Nuclear Physics</em>; Knoll, <em>Radiation Detection and Measurement</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
